---
/* /about: the portrait you can pick up with a hand of clients behind it, the
   longer story, the giant line, two real quotes, and what I do and don't do. */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/pages/about.css';
import Tile from '../components/Tile.astro';
import QuoteBand from '../components/QuoteBand.astro';
import Closing from '../components/Closing.astro';
import OffTheClock from '../components/OffTheClock.astro';
import PhotoStack from '../components/PhotoStack.astro';
import ClientMap from '../components/ClientMap.astro';
import engagement from '../assets/about/family-engagement.jpg';
import picnic from '../assets/about/family-picnic.jpg';
import aquarium from '../assets/about/family-aquarium.jpg';
import shoulders from '../assets/about/family-shoulders.jpg';
import raceBibs from '../assets/about/running-race-bibs.jpg';
import riverfront from '../assets/about/running-riverfront.jpg';
import runSelfie from '../assets/about/running-selfie.jpg';
import { getClients } from '../lib/clients';
import { plainText, wordSpans } from '../lib/words';

const clients = await getClients();
// The hand behind the portrait, in its own order: the middle card shows most.
const hand = [
  'stone-steps-50k',
  'mas-monograms',
  'first-baptist-muncie',
  'theology-matters',
  'presbyterian-academy',
].flatMap((slug) => clients.filter((c) => c.slug === slug));
/* The bio's photo stacks: plain descriptions, no names. */
const familyPhotos = [
  { src: engagement, alt: 'Nathan and his wife laughing by a lake on their wedding day.' },
  { src: picnic, alt: 'Nathan and his wife with their little one on a picnic blanket in a park.' },
  { src: aquarium, alt: 'Nathan, his wife and their little one in a tunnel at an aquarium.' },
  { src: shoulders, alt: "A child in sunglasses riding on Nathan's shoulders on a sunny street." },
];
const runningPhotos = [
  { src: raceBibs, alt: 'Nathan and a friend in race bibs before a local race.' },
  {
    src: riverfront,
    alt: 'Nathan and a friend by the Ohio River before a race, the Cincinnati skyline behind them.',
  },
  { src: runSelfie, alt: 'Nathan in sunglasses and a running shirt, out on a run.' },
];
const heading = `A one-person studio for organizations that <b>take their work seriously.</b>`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Nathan Nixon, web designer | Nixon Creative Studio"
  description="I'm Nathan Nixon, the one person behind Nixon Creative Studio in Cincinnati. I plan, design and build every site myself, and shoot the photos when needed."
>
  <div class="ab" data-ab>
    <div class="ab-text">
      <header class="ab-head">
        <h1 id="ah" aria-label={plainText(heading)} set:html={wordSpans(heading)} />
        <p class="lede">
          I'm Nathan Nixon. I run Nixon Creative Studio out of Cincinnati, Ohio, and I work with
          small businesses, nonprofits, churches and schools, in person around the region and
          anywhere for web work. I do the strategy, the design, the build and, when needed, the
          photography, so the person you meet on day one is the same person who hands you the
          finished site.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="ab-stage-slot" data-slot></div>

      <section class="story" aria-labelledby="sh" data-story>
        <h2 id="sh">The longer story</h2>
        <p>
          I came to this work through a camera. For years I photographed portraits, families,
          weddings, the events people most want to remember, and the part I loved was helping
          someone show up at their best. When the shoots dried up in 2020, I started building
          websites for a few of those same clients, and it turned out to be the same job in a new
          medium: work out what someone is really trying to say, then make it clear. Web design is
          the studio's main work now, but the photographer's eye stayed, and I still shoot the
          photos when a project needs them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before any of that I studied mechanical engineering at the University of Edinburgh, back
          home in Scotland. The degree did not make me a designer, but it taught me to take a thing
          apart, understand why each piece is there, and put it back together so it holds. That is
          how I approach a website too: structure first, surface second, and built so the person who
          inherits it can actually keep it running.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also do plenty of this work as a volunteer. I'm on the media team at Crestview
          Presbyterian Church, where I run the weekly livestream, plan and buy the equipment
          upgrades, and shoot the photo and video the church uses for its website and its socials.
        </p>
        <p data-copy-ignore>
          I moved from Scotland to Cincinnati in 2017 to be with my wife, became a U.S. citizen in
          2022, and these days the two of us are{' '}
          <PhotoStack
            id="family"
            text="raising two kids here"
            label="Family photos"
            photos={familyPhotos}
            tail="."
          />
          When I'm away from a screen I'm usually out on{' '}
          <PhotoStack
            id="running"
            text="a long run"
            label="Running photos"
            photos={runningPhotos}
            tail=","
          />
          chasing landscapes with a camera, deep in an ambitious Lego build with the kids, or a few
          episodes into a history drama.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <div class="ab-stage" data-stage>
      <div class="fan-wins" data-wins>
        {hand.map((s) => (
          <Tile client={s} />
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="fan-wins" data-wins></div>
      <figure class="pcard" data-card>
        <img
          src="/assets/about/portrait-bw.webp"
          srcset="/assets/about/portrait-bw-520.webp 520w, /assets/about/portrait-bw.webp 960w"
          sizes="(max-width: 60rem) min(66vw, 24rem), min(29vw, 44vh, 30rem)"
          width="960"
          height="1200"
          alt="A black-and-white studio portrait of Nathan looking off to the side, one hand behind his neck."
          draggable="false"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
        <figcaption>From the other side of the lens</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sec ab-sec" aria-labelledby="fh">
    <div class="big">
      <h2 id="fh" class="giant" data-weigh data-build style="--fit: 20; --fitm: 20">
        <span>One person.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>
    <dl class="ledger ledger-facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="label">On every project</dt>
        <dd>Strategy, design, the build and, when needed, the photos.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="label">Building websites since 2020</dt>
        <dd>After years behind a camera.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="label">Based in Cincinnati</dt>
        <dd>In person around the region, anywhere for web work.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <ClientMap />

  <section class="sec ab-sec" aria-labelledby="qh">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2 id="qh">In their words</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="says" data-say>
      <QuoteBand as="article" slug="first-baptist-muncie" by="Kendall Ellis" />
      <QuoteBand as="article" flip slug="stone-steps-50k" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec ab-sec" aria-labelledby="dh">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2 id="dh">What I do, and what I don't</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="dodont">
      <div class="card keyed do-card">
        <h3>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M4 10.5 8 14.5 16 5.5" />
          </svg>
          I do
        </h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Plan, design and build your site myself, start to finish.</li>
          <li>Shoot the photos when needed, around the greater Cincinnati region.</li>
          <li>Use your existing brand, or add a simple wordmark.</li>
          <li>
            Build every custom site to the accessibility standard most public bodies use (WCAG 2.2
            AA), so people who use screen readers or have poor eyesight can use it too.
          </li>
          <li>Set it up so the next volunteer or staff member can keep it running.</li>
          <li>
            Stay around after launch. Hosting for the first year is included, and I quote a monthly
            care plan with every build.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card keyed do-card do-ink">
        <h3>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M5 5l10 10M15 5 5 15" />
          </svg>
          I don't
        </h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Pass you along. There's no strategist, designer and developer who never quite talk to
            each other.
          </li>
          <li>Use a stock template. The design is built to your brand.</li>
          <li>Trap you. If you want to move on, the code and content go with you.</li>
          <li>
            Chase the lowest quote. For a very simple need, a cheaper one might be the right call.
          </li>
          <li>
            Shoot photos farther afield. Web work happens anywhere, but photography needs me on
            site.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="tools">
      I use modern tools, AI among them, the same way I use a camera: they help me do the work. The
      strategy, the design and the accountability stay mine, start to finish. What each project
      costs is on <a href="/services/">the prices page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <OffTheClock />

  <Closing lead="Want to talk about <b>a project?</b>" />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/about.js';
  import '../scripts/off-the-clock.js';
  import '../scripts/client-map.js';
</script>
